WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.053
Hi, I'm James Murphy. And today, we're talking&nbsp;about slots in Python.

00:00:04.053 --> 00:00:09.342
Slots are primarily a tool for saving on memory usage when you have a&nbsp;lot of really small objects.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:12.168
Using them is pretty easy. We'll go over that briefly.

00:00:12.168 --> 00:00:14.898
But the main&nbsp;point of this video is to help you understand

00:00:14.898 --> 00:00:18.033
what slots actually are and how they work.

00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:23.135
That&nbsp;means, we're also going to learn a little bit about what by default makes up an instance of a&nbsp;class

00:00:23.135 --> 00:00:29.092
and a little bit about descriptors and a little bit about how classes and instances&nbsp;are laid out in memory.

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:33.464
I'd like to thank me for sponsoring myself.

00:00:33.464 --> 00:00:38.581
Did you know that I am available&nbsp;for consulting, contracting, training and interview prep services?

00:00:38.581 --> 00:00:42.673
I also now accept bitcoin for&nbsp;all business purposes including donations.

00:00:43.694 --> 00:00:46.151
By default, classes act a lot like dictionaries

00:00:46.151 --> 00:00:50.670
except you access attributes with dot syntax&nbsp;instead of using square brackets.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.093
And that's no&nbsp;coincidence.

00:00:53.093 --> 00:00:58.028
Every instance of a normal class has a double under or dunder dict attribute

00:00:58.028 --> 00:01:00.960
where you can actually view this dictionary.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.475
Notice that this dictionary only contains instance&nbsp;attributes.

00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:07.327
So, it contains the x that was assigned on the instance.

00:01:07.327 --> 00:01:10.529
But it does not contain the class&nbsp;variable v.

00:01:10.529 --> 00:01:14.742
The variable v would actually be stored in the capital A class object

00:01:14.742 --> 00:01:18.216
rather than in&nbsp;any little a instance.

00:01:18.216 --> 00:01:22.383
The way that attribute access works is that first Python will look in&nbsp;this dictionary

00:01:22.383 --> 00:01:26.244
and then if the key is found, it will return you that value.

00:01:26.244 --> 00:01:29.111
If Python can't find&nbsp;what it's looking for in the instance dictionary,

00:01:29.111 --> 00:01:33.636
then it'll start looking in the parent classes and their parent classes and so on all the way up the chain

00:01:33.636 --> 00:01:36.254
or raise an attribute error if it&nbsp;doesn't find anything.

00:01:36.254 --> 00:01:42.173
So, in this case, a.v is found in the capital A's dictionary instead of&nbsp;the little a's dictionary.

00:01:42.173 --> 00:01:46.259
You get an error if you try to access something that's not in any of the&nbsp;parent classes.

00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:48.777
But you can add new attributes at runtime

00:01:48.777 --> 00:01:50.913
because dictionaries are dynamically&nbsp;allocated

00:01:50.913 --> 00:01:54.542
and you can add new elements to them no problem.

00:01:54.542 --> 00:01:56.458
Now let's define a class that uses&nbsp;slots.

00:01:56.458 --> 00:02:00.861
It's very simple. You just add a dunder slots attribute onto the class.

00:02:00.861 --> 00:02:03.338
It should just be a&nbsp;tuple of your instance attributes.

00:02:03.338 --> 00:02:05.937
In this case, our only instance attribute is x.

00:02:05.937 --> 00:02:09.295
v is a class variable&nbsp;not an instance variable.

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:14.907
The first thing to note about using a slotted class is that instances no&nbsp;longer have this dunder dict attribute.

00:02:14.907 --> 00:02:16.423
Instead of using a dictionary,

00:02:16.423 --> 00:02:20.563
Python will just create the&nbsp;space for each slot directly on each instance.

00:02:20.563 --> 00:02:24.191
You can still look up slotted variables and change&nbsp;them as usual.

00:02:24.191 --> 00:02:26.807
Plotting does not affect mutability.

00:02:26.807 --> 00:02:32.850
The main difference in functionality with a&nbsp;slotted class is that you can no longer add&nbsp;new attributes at runtime.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:36.280
All the attributes are&nbsp;decided when the class is defined.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.432
However, keep in mind that the class is different than an instance.

00:02:39.432 --> 00:02:42.542
So, I can still set class variables on the class

00:02:42.542 --> 00:02:45.089
even though I couldn't set it on the instance.

00:02:45.089 --> 00:02:48.332
So, it seems that slotted classes are just less functional classes.

00:02:48.332 --> 00:02:50.352
Why would you ever&nbsp;want to use one?

00:02:50.352 --> 00:02:51.944
Let's take a look at the memory usage.

00:02:51.944 --> 00:02:57.265
Here, I'm using sys.getsizeof() to get&nbsp;the size of an instance of A and an instance of B.

00:02:57.265 --> 00:03:00.112
Here, A did not use slots and B did.

00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:04.041
So, the A&nbsp;instance uses 48 bytes and the B instance uses 40 bytes.

00:03:04.041 --> 00:03:05.822
Okay. So, that's a little bit less memory.

00:03:05.822 --> 00:03:08.751
But&nbsp;sys.getsizeof() doesn't give us the full picture.

00:03:08.751 --> 00:03:12.705
sys.getsizeof() doesn't count the size of&nbsp;sub-objects within an object.

00:03:12.705 --> 00:03:16.103
So, it's completely ignoring the actual size of the dictionary.

00:03:16.103 --> 00:03:22.883
If&nbsp;instead I use a recursive getsize() function that actually counts the size of sub-objects and their&nbsp;sub-objects and so on,

00:03:22.883 --> 00:03:25.039
then we see much bigger numbers.

00:03:25.039 --> 00:03:29.733
The normal class used 230 bytes whereas&nbsp;the slotted class only used 68.

00:03:29.733 --> 00:03:33.807
That's a factor of three savings just for knowing the names of&nbsp;your variables ahead of time.

00:03:33.807 --> 00:03:34.863
Is it worth it?

00:03:34.863 --> 00:03:37.796
The difference gets even bigger the more attributes&nbsp;you have.

00:03:37.796 --> 00:03:39.731
In this case, we have seven attributes.

00:03:39.731 --> 00:03:43.349
And we compare not slotted versus slotted and&nbsp;these are the results.

00:03:43.349 --> 00:03:47.649
With seven attributes which I'd say is an upper middle amount for an average&nbsp;class,

00:03:47.649 --> 00:03:50.720
we have a nearly five times memory savings.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.658
Personally, I'm rarely memory constrained in&nbsp;Python.

00:03:53.658 --> 00:03:57.669
I have 32 gigs of RAM and I don't usually work with data that big.

00:03:57.669 --> 00:04:01.521
But in the rare case that&nbsp;I'm actually using lots and lots of small objects,

00:04:01.521 --> 00:04:05.187
a five times memory savings is a pretty good&nbsp;deal.

00:04:05.187 --> 00:04:09.330
Thankfully if I'm using something like a named tuple, this happens automatically.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:14.401
I also&nbsp;tested for speed differences in creating objects, getting attributes and setting attributes

00:04:14.401 --> 00:04:17.040
on slotted versus not slotted classes.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.151
I found slotted classes to be faster.

00:04:19.151 --> 00:04:22.279
But just a&nbsp;little bit. Nothing to redesign your code around.

00:04:22.279 --> 00:04:25.737
So, if instances of slotted classes don't use instance&nbsp;dictionaries,

00:04:25.737 --> 00:04:27.780
then how do slots actually work?

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.000
Here, I have a not slotted and slotted class.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.374
And then&nbsp;I print out the dictionaries of both the classes.

00:04:33.374 --> 00:04:36.840
Note that it's the instances of slotted classes&nbsp;that don't have dictionaries.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.529
The class itself still has a dictionary.

00:04:39.529 --> 00:04:43.549
Notice that, the class&nbsp;object capital B already has an x attribute

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:45.537
even though I've never assigned to it.

00:04:45.537 --> 00:04:47.532
Well, printing&nbsp;it out didn't really tell us much.

00:04:47.532 --> 00:04:51.116
But it turns out that this object is actually a descriptor.

00:04:51.117 --> 00:04:56.640
Descriptors which you could write yourself allow you to modify the way that attribute access&nbsp;happens.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.239
When Python looks up an object, if it finds that that object has a get method,

00:05:00.239 --> 00:05:03.847
it'll actually&nbsp;call that method instead of just returning the object.

00:05:03.847 --> 00:05:06.074
When I try to access little b.x,

00:05:06.074 --> 00:05:08.628
it doesn't&nbsp;find the x in the little b object.

00:05:08.628 --> 00:05:10.828
So, it looks in the super class.

00:05:10.828 --> 00:05:12.923
Since capital B.x has a&nbsp;get method,

00:05:12.923 --> 00:05:18.206
it goes ahead and calls it with the instance being the little b and the owner&nbsp;being the big B.

00:05:18.206 --> 00:05:20.429
The real number descriptor is written in C

00:05:20.429 --> 00:05:24.794
and it will directly reach into the&nbsp;underlying memory underneath the instance object

00:05:24.794 --> 00:05:26.752
and return the value of x.

00:05:26.752 --> 00:05:28.810
It doesn't have to&nbsp;look up x in a dictionary.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:35.974
It just remembers that all instances of capital B's store their x's&nbsp;at a fixed offset relative to the base instance.

00:05:35.974 --> 00:05:37.559
We'll see more on this in a bit.

00:05:37.559 --> 00:05:40.722
And a&nbsp;similar thing happens when you try to assign to x.

00:05:40.722 --> 00:05:46.000
It calls the set. And then, it uses C to&nbsp;set the value at a fixed offset within the object.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.075
Okay. So, there's some descriptor magic going on,&nbsp;some stuff that's happening in C.

00:05:50.075 --> 00:05:52.741
But what actually is a slot?

00:05:52.741 --> 00:05:56.289
To understand what a slot actually is&nbsp;we need to enter the matrix.

00:05:56.289 --> 00:06:00.380
We need to understand how Python objects are laid out in memory.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:04.450
The&nbsp;actual numbers that we're looking at may depend a little bit on the architecture of your computer.

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:09.167
On my computer, sizes take up 8 bytes and pointers take up 8 bytes.

00:06:09.167 --> 00:06:12.791
So, here I have a slotted class&nbsp;A with no slots.

00:06:12.791 --> 00:06:15.952
Every instance of A is laid out in memory like this.

00:06:15.952 --> 00:06:20.720
All Python objects start out&nbsp;with a reference count and a pointer to their type.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.257
They could have other things. But for this class&nbsp;A, there's nothing else.

00:06:24.257 --> 00:06:28.833
The basicsize of a class tells you how much memory an instance of the class&nbsp;takes.

00:06:28.833 --> 00:06:31.297
Our class A is just a size and a pointer,

00:06:31.297 --> 00:06:33.729
the reference count and a pointer to the type.

00:06:33.729 --> 00:06:35.206
So, that's 16 bytes.

00:06:35.206 --> 00:06:39.394
And when I run it, we see that indeed A's basicsize is 16.

00:06:39.394 --> 00:06:44.717
Similar to sys.getsizeof(), basicsize doesn't include the size of any sub-objects.

00:06:44.717 --> 00:06:48.146
It's basically just counting up the&nbsp;size of the pointers and the size.

00:06:48.146 --> 00:06:51.658
sys.getsizeof() actually counts a little bit more though.

00:06:51.658 --> 00:06:53.549
For the&nbsp;purpose of garbage collection,

00:06:53.549 --> 00:06:57.536
Python actually puts two pointers just before the object starts.

00:06:57.536 --> 00:07:00.689
And&nbsp;getsizeof() actually counts those two pointers.

00:07:00.689 --> 00:07:05.989
So, sys.getsizeof() is going to count 1, 2, 3 pointers and 1 size.

00:07:05.989 --> 00:07:08.543
That's four times eight is 32 bytes.

00:07:08.543 --> 00:07:11.024
And that's exactly what we&nbsp;see when we run it.

00:07:11.024 --> 00:07:12.182
But these pictures are big enough.

00:07:12.182 --> 00:07:15.664
So, let's just focus on the instance&nbsp;and ignore the garbage collection stuff.

00:07:15.664 --> 00:07:18.511
The last class defined the slot's variable&nbsp;but it was empty.

00:07:18.511 --> 00:07:20.888
Here's what happens if you actually define slots.

00:07:20.888 --> 00:07:24.916
Again all objects start with&nbsp;their reference count and a pointer to their type.

00:07:24.916 --> 00:07:27.995
Directly following that is just a pointer for each&nbsp;slot.

00:07:27.995 --> 00:07:29.568
So, that's all the slot is.

00:07:29.568 --> 00:07:31.694
A slot is really just a piece of memory

00:07:31.694 --> 00:07:35.067
that you use for storing&nbsp;a particular piece of data about the instance.

00:07:35.067 --> 00:07:38.997
This slot holds x. This slot holds the reference&nbsp;count and this one the type.

00:07:38.997 --> 00:07:44.711
So, really you should think of the dunder slots variable as naming&nbsp;the extra slots that this class defines.

00:07:44.711 --> 00:07:47.745
This is also in line with how it actually works with&nbsp;inheritance.

00:07:47.745 --> 00:07:50.866
So, for each instance, we have one size and four pointers.

00:07:50.866 --> 00:07:53.317
That's five times eight is 40&nbsp;bytes.

00:07:53.317 --> 00:07:57.997
If you include the garbage collection stuff, then that's 16 more bytes and you get 56.

00:07:57.997 --> 00:08:01.040
Let's&nbsp;look at the layout of just a normal class now.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:04.112
As always, we have the reference count and pointer&nbsp;to the type.

00:08:04.112 --> 00:08:06.802
Then instead of having a space for x, y and z directly,

00:08:06.802 --> 00:08:09.324
we have a space for the instance&nbsp;dictionary.

00:08:09.324 --> 00:08:12.255
x, y and z don't actually show up anywhere in the instance.

00:08:12.255 --> 00:08:16.668
You have to look inside&nbsp;the data for the dictionary and that's where you would find x, y and z.

00:08:16.668 --> 00:08:18.482
After they're initialized,&nbsp;of course.

00:08:18.482 --> 00:08:20.388
There's also going to be this weak reference attribute

00:08:20.389 --> 00:08:23.687
which would be part of the&nbsp;object by default if you didn't define slots.

00:08:23.687 --> 00:08:25.867
Explaining weakref is a topic for a different&nbsp;video.

00:08:25.867 --> 00:08:28.209
Just know that defining slots gets rid of it.

00:08:28.209 --> 00:08:32.705
Go ahead and make sure you can explain why the&nbsp;basic size of this object is 32 bytes.

00:08:32.705 --> 00:08:33.801
So, what about inheritance?

00:08:33.801 --> 00:08:38.862
What happens if you inherit from&nbsp;a class that defines slots but that class doesn't itself define slots?

00:08:38.862 --> 00:08:41.157
Well, being a slotted class is&nbsp;not inherited.

00:08:41.157 --> 00:08:46.642
So, even if B inherits from A which defines slots, B will get an instance dictionary.

00:08:46.642 --> 00:08:50.614
But remember, you should think about the dunder slots variable as being extra slots.

00:08:50.614 --> 00:08:54.352
So, B didn't&nbsp;define any extra slots. But it still has the ones from A.

00:08:54.352 --> 00:08:59.600
So, if I define b dot x equals 10, that&nbsp;will not show up in the instance dictionary of b.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.419
The x variable was a designated slot from A.

00:09:02.419 --> 00:09:05.680
So, it will get stored in its designated spot from A.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:08.060
If I do want to define more slots in a subclass,

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:09.914
I should only include the additional ones.

00:09:09.914 --> 00:09:15.683
So, in this case, I add a t slot and the x, y and z&nbsp;ones will still be there from A.

00:09:15.683 --> 00:09:21.120
For anyone wondering about metaclasses, you will get an&nbsp;error if you try to define slots on a metaclass.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.841
You can make it have empty slots though.

00:09:23.841 --> 00:09:27.760
Finally,&nbsp;you can actually define dict as one of the slots.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:31.071
If you do, instances will get instance dictionaries.

00:09:31.071 --> 00:09:35.040
In this case, instances will have a dict.&nbsp;But they won't have that weakref attribute.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.594
I could also define a weakref slot if I wanted&nbsp;weakrefs but not dictionaries.

00:09:39.594 --> 00:09:44.302
As we saw before, empty slots will prevent the creation of both weakrefs and dictionaries.

00:09:44.302 --> 00:09:48.618
And if you don't define slots, then instances will have both weakrefs and dictionaries.

00:09:48.618 --> 00:09:52.911
The last case is if you have something like a dictionary along with additional&nbsp;slots.

00:09:52.911 --> 00:09:55.635
In this case, objects will have instance dictionaries.

00:09:55.635 --> 00:10:00.960
But specifically, the attributes x&nbsp;and y won't be stored in the instance dictionary.

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:04.039
The legitimate use cases for doing something like&nbsp;this are pretty slim.

00:10:04.039 --> 00:10:05.834
But that's the way it works.

00:10:05.834 --> 00:10:15.700
As always, thank you for watching and don't forget&nbsp;to slap that like button an odd number of times.

